From 07cd46b401ba51f352a171ddf4e2602894447532 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 18:00:54 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent: ship Claude Agent (Plus) and Claude Agent TUI as
 default agents

Zeo exists to carry the Claude agent adapters, yet a fresh install offered
neither: an agent appears in the thread menu only through an `agent_servers`
entry, and Zeo's defaults had none. Every Gentoo user had to write those
entries by hand, and the Flatpak -- which keeps its own settings under
~/.var/app/dev.zeo.Zeo -- started without them even on a machine whose native
Zeo had them. Found on 2026-10-03 testing the Flatpak.

Zed already has the mechanism for agents that need no setup: the ACP registry.
A registry entry names an npm package; on first use Zed installs it with its
own Node runtime into external_agents_dir/registry/npx/<id> and launches its
bin. That is how claude-acp works out of the box. The adapters are on npm, so
this uses the same path rather than a new one:

  agent_registry_store.rs adds two npx entries, claude-agent-acp-plus and
  claude-agent-tui, to whatever the registry returned -- and to an empty
  list, so they exist before the first fetch and offline. A registry entry
  with the same id wins over Zeo's.

  default.json enables both as `{ "type": "registry" }` under agent_servers,
  so they are thread options with no configuration. User settings merge on
  top, as for any default.

The packages are `@lucascouts/...@latest`, by decision: every launch runs
`npm install <package>@latest`, so the adapters track the newest published
version without a Zeo rebuild. bounded_npm_package_spec already leaves a
non-semver tag alone, so the spec reaches npm unchanged. The trade is that no
release-age cooldown applies to these two packages.

Claude Agent TUI runs the real `claude` CLI in a terminal, so it still needs
that CLI on the system; its description says so. Claude Agent (Plus) bundles
its CLI and needs nothing.

Tests: the two entries exist with no registry, a registry entry with the same
id is kept instead of duplicated, `@latest` reaches npm intact, and
SettingsStore::test reports both enabled as registry agents.

Never-empty list (fixed 2026-10-09): `AgentRegistryStore::init_global` fetched
the registry only `if store.agents.is_empty()`, which `with_zeo_agents` made
permanently false -- the store's own first fetch never ran. In a running Zeo
nothing showed it, because default.json's two registry agents make
agent_server_store call `refresh_if_stale` at startup anyway; the three
`registry_refresh_*` integration tests, which drive `init_global` alone, did.
The guard is now "nothing but Zeo's built-ins" (`is_zeo_agent`). The tests that
count agents expect the two built-ins after the registry's:
`registry_refresh_does_not_block_sequentially_on_hung_icon_downloads` (3 + 2)
and remote_server's `test_remote_external_agent_server` ("foo" plus both ids).
---

